<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Koch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Heinrich Hermann Robert Koch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Koch was a German physician and microbiologist who is regarded as one of the main founders of modern bacteriology. As a private physician he identified the anthrax bacterium in 1876; he announced the tuberculosis bacterium on 24 March 1882 and isolated the cholera bacterium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> in 1884. His culture method using agar and glass plates, later developed into the Petri dish by his assistant Julius Richard Petri, and the principles known as Koch's postulates gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germ-theory-of-disease">germ theory</a> of disease its proofs. His tuberculin, announced as a cure in 1890, proved ineffective and damaged his standing. He won the 1905 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physiology or Medicine and died in Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-koch.jpg" alt="Robert Koch" width="330" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertKoch_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
